<%# THE PROFILE LAB PAGE — the two browser programs /profile and /profile/edit add.

    Both are invisible to every tier this repo already runs, and invisible in the
    same way: the server emits identical bytes whether the program works or not.

      1. studio/profiles/_identity_mini carries an IntersectionObserver that
         reveals a compact header once the full card has mostly scrolled past. A
         String assertion sees the bar's markup on every page — it is always in
         the document — and can say nothing about whether it EVER appears, or
         whether it appears at the right moment. `is-visible` is toggled by an
         observer callback, and its effect is a computed opacity.

      2. studio/profiles/_form_script's dirty check raises the save bar. The bar
         is likewise always in the markup, behind x-show, and a markup test
         asserting `x-show="dirty"` stays green while the component throws on
         init, computes `changed()` wrongly, or never boots at all.

    This page renders BOTH on one document deliberately: on the real edit page
    they coexist, the mini bar is fixed at the top while the save bar is fixed at
    the bottom, and a spec that scrolls to dirty a field is exercising the two
    together — which is the composition a consumer actually ships.

    LOCALS ONLY. Per test/lib/e2e_lane_contract_test.rb this page may set a
    partial's locals and nothing else. The save bar is rendered by name for that
    reason: it lived inline in edit.html.erb and was extracted into
    a partial so there was a name to render, rather than copied here — a copy
    would make the spec a test of the lab. (The save controls themselves have
    since moved into the identity card and off this page, which renders the READ
    identity and has nothing to save.)

    THE SPACER is layout, not behaviour: the observer needs somewhere to scroll
    to, and a viewport-height column of nothing is the cheapest honest way to
    give it one. It asserts nothing and no partial reimplements it. %>
<div x-data="studioProfileForm(<%= { first_name: @user.first_name, last_name: @user.last_name, email: @user.email }.to_json %>)">
  <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto py-8">
    <%= render "studio/profiles/identity", user: @user, editable: false %>

    <%# The newsletter row and the host its confirmation opens from. Rendered
        through the RESOLVED sections rather than by name, because the thing under
        test is partly the resolution: the host must mount because a row DECLARED
        modals, not because this page hard-coded one. %>
    <div class="card p-0 overflow-hidden mb-6">
      <% @profile_sections.each do |section| %>
        <section class="p-6" data-profile-section="<%= section[:key] %>">
          <%= render section[:partial], **section.fetch(:locals, {}).merge(user: @user) %>
        </section>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <form x-ref="form" data-studio-lab-form>
      <div class="card p-6">
        <%= render "studio/profiles/name_fields", user: @user %>
      </div>
    </form>

    <%# Scroll runway. The observer hands over at 60% of the card, so the page
        has to be tall enough for the card to actually get there. %>
    <div data-studio-lab-spacer style="height: 300vh;"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<% modal_rows = @profile_sections.select { |section| section[:modals] } %>
<% if modal_rows.any? %>
  <%# The x-data wrapper is the page's job, not the host's — see show.html.erb.
      The lab reproduces it because a lab page that got this right by accident
      would test a shape no consumer renders. %>
  <div x-data>
    <%= render "studio/modals/scoped_host", store: "profileModals" do %>
      <% modal_rows.each do |section| %>
        <%= render section[:modals], user: @user %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>

<%= render "studio/profiles/form_script" %>
